# Template for .env.local — this file IS committed, so it must never contain
# real values. Copy it to .env.local and fill them in.
#
# THE RULE THAT MATTERS: only VITE_ variables reach the browser. Vite compiles
# them into the bundle, where anyone can read them. Everything else stays on
# the API server. `npm run security-check` proves the secrets below are absent
# from the built bundle.

# --- Browser (public, protected by Row-Level Security) ----------------------
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co
VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# --- Server only. Never prefix any of these with VITE_ ----------------------

# Direct Postgres connection, used ONLY by `npm run apply-db` to build a fresh
# database from db/*.sql. Nothing the app serves ever reads it.
#
# Supabase → Connect → Session pooler. Take the SESSION pooler (port 5432), not
# the transaction pooler (6543): schema changes need a connection that stays
# yours between statements. Replace [YOUR-PASSWORD] with the database password
# you chose when the project was created — it is NOT any of the keys below.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres.your-project-ref:YOUR-PASSWORD@aws-0-ap-southeast-2.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres

# Supabase → Project Settings → API Keys → service_role / secret.
# BYPASSES every RLS policy. Treat it like the database password.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=sb_secret_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# console.anthropic.com → API keys.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# --- Stripe: TWO different secrets, easily confused -------------------------
# They are not interchangeable, and pasting one into the other's name fails
# only when somebody presses Pay. The server checks the prefixes at startup.

# Stripe dashboard → Developers → API keys. Authorises calls TO Stripe.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# --- Email. Optional: without it, links are copied by hand instead ----------
# resend.com -> API Keys. Free tier is 3,000 messages a month.
#
# WITHOUT A VERIFIED DOMAIN Resend only delivers to the address that owns the
# account. That is enough to test the whole flow; it is not enough to email a
# real school. Verifying a domain is the step that changes.
RESEND_API_KEY=re_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# Who the message comes from. Must be on a domain verified with the provider,
# except for onboarding@resend.dev which only reaches your own address.
MAIL_FROM=MiZanova <onboarding@resend.dev>

# --- OR: Gmail SMTP, which is what reaches a real tester ---------------------
# THE SECOND WAY TO SEND, and the one to use before a domain exists. Resend's
# sandbox sender above is refused for every recipient except the account
# holder's own address, so it cannot invite anybody. An ordinary Gmail account
# with an App Password sends to anyone, about 500 a day, for nothing.
#
# SMTP WINS WHEN BOTH ARE SET. server/mail.js checks SMTP first, and a
# MAIL_FROM pointing at resend.dev is ignored while SMTP is on — so the two
# lines above can stay exactly as they are. Nothing needs deleting.
#
# IT CANNOT WORK AT ALL ON RENDER'S FREE TIER. Since 26 Sep 2025 free web
# services block outbound traffic to SMTP ports 25, 465 AND 587, so every port
# is a dead end and the symptom is a bare `Connection timeout`. Any paid
# instance lifts it (25 stays blocked everywhere). This is worth reading twice
# before spending another evening on credentials: the four Connection timeout
# rows in system_events between 13 Aug and 1 Sep are all this, and nothing in
# the error says so. Resend is unaffected — it is an HTTPS API on 443.
#
# THE ONE THAT ACTUALLY BIT US, twice, and looks like none of the below:
#   `connect ENETUNREACH 2404:6800:4003:...:465` — smtp.gmail.com resolves to
#   an IPv6 address, Node prefers it since v17, and Render has no routable
#   IPv6. server/mail.js pins `family: 4` to fix it. Recorded in system_events
#   on 22 Aug (port 587) and 1 Sep (port 465) before anybody read the error.
#
# THREE THINGS THAT MAKE THIS FAIL, all of them outside the code:
#   1. It must be a PERSONAL @gmail.com. Google Workspace accounts — including
#      a university or school address — commonly have App Passwords disabled by
#      an administrator, and nothing here can work around that.
#   2. 2-Step Verification must be ON first, or the App passwords page does not
#      exist and the ordinary password gets used instead. Google stopped
#      accepting those in 2022; the symptom is a 535 auth error.
#   3. Google displays the App Password as `abcd efgh ijkl mnop`. The spaces
#      are for reading, not for pasting. Strip them.
#
# myaccount.google.com -> Security -> 2-Step Verification -> App passwords.
# Prove it with `npm run mail-check you@example.com`, which prints what the
# provider actually said rather than reporting an attempt as a success.
SMTP_USER=you@gmail.com
SMTP_PASS=PASTE_YOUR_16_CHARACTER_APP_PASSWORD

# Defaults, set only if 465 is blocked outbound — some hosts do that. Port 587
# is the usual alternative; `secure` follows the port automatically.
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com
# SMTP_PORT=465

# Printed by `stripe listen --forward-to localhost:8887/api/billing/webhook`.
# Verifies that a payment notification really came FROM Stripe. Without it the
# webhook refuses everything, which is safe but means payments are only
# recorded when the parent's browser comes back.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
